#pragma once

// What the right half of the printer/filament chip says about the project's
// filament slots: one dot per slot, then a label.
//
// Kept apart from the widget so the rules -- which dots are full, which fade,
// when unused slots fold into "+n", when the label names a filament and when
// it counts them -- are plain data that tests can read without wx or Orca.

#include <cstddef>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

namespace Slic3r::GUI::JusPrin {

// One project slot, as the chip needs it.
struct ChipSlot
{
    std::string preset; // the filament preset; slots sharing one print the same filament
    std::string name;   // its short name
    std::string colour; // "#RRGGBB", or empty
    bool        used{false};
};

struct ChipDot
{
    std::size_t slot{0}; // 0-based
    std::string colour;
    bool        faded{false};
};

struct FilamentChipModel
{
    // In slot order.
    std::vector<ChipDot> dots;
    // Unused slots folded into a grey "+n" after the dots; zero draws nothing.
    std::size_t folded{0};
    // More slots in use than fit at full size: every one keeps its dot, and
    // the dots shrink.
    bool shrunk{false};
    // The short name when the slots in use share one filament; empty when the
    // label is a count instead.
    std::string name;
    // How many different filaments the slots in use print with.
    std::size_t filaments{0};
};

// Up to this many dots draw at full size.
constexpr std::size_t kFullSizeDots = 8;

// A plate with nothing on it uses no slot, and a chip of faded dots over a
// count would say nothing about the project. Every slot then reads as in use.
FilamentChipModel describe_filament_chip(const std::vector<ChipSlot>& slots);

} // namespace Slic3r::GUI::JusPrin
----- chip
3:// The Prepare header's machine chip: printer on the left, filament on the
4:// right, one outline around both.
5://
6:// The halves are two HeaderButtons rather than one control with two hit
7:// regions, so each gets its own hover, pressed, focus and open state for free,
8:// and each can anchor its own menu. Left and right arrow keys move between
9:// them, so the chip behaves like one control to the keyboard while remaining
10:// two to the pointer.
11://
12:// The chip renders state and raises intent. It reads nothing and changes
13:// nothing in Orca; StatusRow owns both. It states facts and never warns.
30:    // Left half. `nozzle` is already formatted for display; `tooltip` is the
31:    // full text the half may ellipsize.
33:    // Right half: the slot dots and label the model describes. `label` is the
34:    // text the model resolved to -- a short name or a count.
39:    // Raised when a half is activated, by pointer or keyboard.
47:    // The whole chip as one image: both halves drawn side by side exactly as
48:    // they sit in the header.
60:} // namespace Slic3r::GUI::JusPrin
----- printer menu
3:// The chip's left-half menu: what this printer is, what it is doing, its
4:// nozzle (shown, not changed), and the plate, the one setup value a person
5:// changes often enough to want here. Everything else is a door into Orca's
6:// own settings.
7://
8:// The selected printer stays at the top. Other saved printers follow its
9:// nozzle and plate rows, with the same live status Home can report. Connecting
10:// a non-Bambu print host remains part of printer setup.
11://
12:// The plate sub-list replaces the menu's own rows instead of opening a second
13:// popup, so there is one surface to dismiss and one keyboard model.
28:    // Opens the menu anchored to the chip's left half.
29:    //
30:    // Lifetime: HeaderMenu closes the popup *before* running a row's callback,
31:    // so a raw controller would be freed while a queued callback still pointed
